      "claim": "The query's very low logD (-2.72) and full ionization limit direct transferability, and no direct F measurement for the query is available; however, no affirmative threshold-relevant evidence supports F < 20%.",
      "card_ids": []
    }
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [
    {
      "family": "gut_wall_efflux_intestinal_metabolism",
      "applicability": "moderate",
      "direction": "supportive",
      "decision_effect": "strengthened",
      "card_ids": ["C54","C55","C56","C57","C58","C59","C60","C61","C62","C63","C64","C65"]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct experimental absolute oral bioavailability measurement for the query molecule is available in the supplied records.",
    "No query-specific gut-wall efflux or intestinal-metabolism data are available; L5 records are for reference molecules and are not independent gold votes.",
    "The query's estimated logD (-2.72) is much lower than most reference acids (naproxen, ketoprofen, fenoprofen), limiting transferability of their high fraction-absorbed results.",
    "Hepatic first-pass metabolism and metabolic stability for the query are not experimentally established.",
    "Active transport or paracellular absorption for the query cannot be ruled out without experimental data.",
    "The L5 family contains permeability/efflux records but no direct intestinal-metabolism measurements."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "L5 gut-wall efflux records show no efflux liability for acidic reference molecules and high bidirectional permeability, adding mechanistic support for absorption; combined with direct L1 F values for ionized/acidic analogs (neotame ≥34.3%, 6-MNA 35-38%, tolfenamic acid 91-100%) and L4 high fraction absorbed for carboxylic acids, the high prediction is strengthened with moderate confidence."
}

